      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Spontaneous spinal epidural hematoma (SSEH) is a rare spinal cord disorder requiring urgent diagnosis and prompt treatment to prevent irreversible neurological damage. Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH), usually presenting with headache and neck stiffness, is also a worldwide neurosurgical emergency. In this case study, a patient on clopidogrel presented with an SSEH mimicking a spontaneous SAH."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE DESCRIPTION",
          "content": "A 74-year-old female presented with severe headache and neck pain which ultimately attributed to a cervicothoracic SSEH resulting in cord compression. Notably, there was a long delay in establishing the diagnosis of SSEH as her symptoms mimicked a SAH. Although the patient ultimately underwent spinal surgery followed by intense rehabilitation, the diagnostic delay resulted in a poor neurological outcome."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Patients with spontaneous cervicothoracic epidural hematomas may occasionally present with symptoms of severe headache and neck pain/stiffness mimicking an SAH. Long diagnostic delays in establishing SSEH may result in irreversible cord damage."
        }
      ]},
